Special Conditions for Electronic Advertising and Digital Boards

5 - 1 It is prohibited to place moving advertisements the content of which includes full-motion video.

5 - 2 It is permitted to place electronic or digital boards (LED) (with the exception of boards of Category (A)), and they are subject to the same conditions as other advertisements, and all the provisions of this Decree shall apply to them in respect of measurements, installation sites, rights, duties and prohibitions.

5 - 3 Special conditions for electronic or digital boards (LED):

5 - 3 - 1 (LED) boards installed on public roads where they are seen by motor vehicle drivers may display static or animated (Animation) advertisements

without their content including full-motion video, flashing or warning lights;

5 - 3 - 2 It is prohibited to convert (LED) boards into a television screen;

5 - 3 - 3 The duration of each advertisement on (LED) screens cannot be less than 8 seconds;

5 - 3 - 4 The board must be equipped with a device that adjusts the lighting in a manner that does not dazzle the eyes of drivers at night, such that the light rate does not exceed 5000 lux (lumens/ m2) in broad daylight, and 2000 lux (lumens/ m2) at night;

5 - 3 - 5 Exempted from these conditions are pharmacies, which are urged only to place the emblem of the pharmacy profession with the word "Pharmacy" without any trade name.

Licence Application

7 - 1 The licence applicant must be a commercial company or establishment duly registered, with the exception of licence applicants in the cases provided for in items /2 - 4/, /2 - 5/ and /2 - 6/ of the second Article of this Decree.

7 - 2 The application shall be submitted to the Head of the Executive Authority in the municipality within the municipal boundaries and to each of the Governor (in the central district) or the Kaymakam (in the district) outside the municipal boundaries, provided that it includes:

7 - 2 - 1 The name and capacity of its submitter, his full address, his telephone number, the real estate area in which the advertisement is intended to be placed, the property number, and a commercial circular proving that the submitter of the application is a company engaged in road advertising, subject to the provisions of item /7 - 1/ of this Article;

7 - 2 - 2 A real estate certificate for the property in which the advertisement will be placed dating back no more than three months (in the event that the sign to be installed is within private property);

7 - 2 - 3 A planning and easement certificate for the location of the sign dating back no more than three months;

7 - 2 - 4 A third-party liability insurance policy for the sign, provided that the signs are insured up to the following amounts:

-/150 000 000/ L.L. (one hundred and fifty million Lebanese Pounds) for material damages;

-/150 000 000/ L.L. (one hundred and fifty million Lebanese Pounds) for human damages;

-/750 000 000/ L.L. (seven hundred and fifty million Lebanese Pounds) total material and bodily damages per accident;

7 - 2 - 5 A document proving that the advertisement is not illuminated by means of infringement on power networks;

7 - 2 - 6 If the sign is within private property, an approval from the owner of the property registered with the Notary Public or a contract with the owner of the property duly registered with the Notary Public and the municipality within the municipal boundaries or the Kaymakamate (in the district) outside the municipal boundaries shall be attached;

If the sign is within the property of public administrations and autonomous agencies, an approval from the competent administration shall be attached, provided that this administration observes the provisions of this Decree;

If the sign is in public property within the municipal boundaries, including road setbacks not equipped with pavements, the decision shall rest with the Mayor of the municipality, subject to the provisions of this Decree and the Building Law;

7 - 2 - 1 One copy of the site map at a scale of 1/100 and of the advertising sign map at a scale of 1/20 showing the dimensions of the sign, its height above the ground, the method of its fixation and the materials from which it is made;

7 - 2 - 2 One copy of the building facade plan at a scale of 1/50 showing the lines of the building and how the advertisement is installed thereon;

7 - 2 - 3 A photograph of the site showing the road and the surrounding environment, with the obligation to indicate the actual location of the sign on the photograph;

7 - 2 - 4 In respect of signs on building rooftops (Rooftop Sign), signs of Category (D) and signs on building facades (Wall Mural) installed in the form of

{Scaffolding}, an engineering study of the structure and the chassis signed by the responsible engineer who will supervise the execution and under his responsibility must be submitted;

7 - 2 - 5 An on-demand bank guarantee in accordance with the provisions of the tenth Article of this Decree.

7 - 3 The licence application submitted to the Vehicles and Machinery Registration Department for placing advertisements on all vehicles and machinery must include:

The name and capacity of its submitter, his full address, and his telephone number if available;

A copy of the vehicle circulation permit;

A third-party liability insurance policy for the vehicle;

An undertaking drawn up before the Notary Public to respect the technical specifications provided for in item /2 - 18/ of the second Article of this Decree.

7 - 4 The Vehicles and Machinery Registration Department shall grant the licence to place advertisements on all vehicles and machinery, subject to not violating the Traffic Law and respecting the technical specifications provided for in item /2 - 18/ of the second Article of this Decree.

7 - 5 Every application that does not fulfil all the conditions provided for under this Decree, or if the licence applicant owns any sign violating the provisions of this Decree, shall be rejected.

Terms and Definitions:

The following words and expressions, wherever they appear in this Law, shall have the following meanings:

1. The Ministry: The Ministry of Education and Higher Education;

2. The Minister: The Minister of Education and Higher Education;

3. The Council: The Council of Higher Education;

4. The Academic Technical Committee: The committee tasked with examining the files attached to applications relating to private institutions of higher education, as commissioned by the Council in accordance with the tasks assigned to it in this Law;

5. The Recognition and Equivalence Committee: The Committee for the Recognition and Equivalence of Higher Education Studies and Degrees;

6. The Secondary Certificate: The certificate that permits admission to institutions of higher education, either through the Lebanese General Secondary Certificate or its official equivalent, or through the Lebanese Technical Baccalaureate Certificate or its official equivalent in accordance with specified pathways issued by a decision of the Minister of Education and Higher Education upon a recommendation from the Recognition and Equivalence Committee;

7. Higher Education: Education that follows the holding of the Secondary Certificate;

8. The Campus: Every main or branch geographical seat established by an institution of higher education in order to carry out its academic activity therein;

9. The Academic Units: The faculties or institutes

10. The Teaching Body: All personnel working at an institution of higher education tasked with academic duties of teaching and supervision or research;

11. The Course: A set of lessons and teaching and learning activities pertaining to a specific academic subject, which constitutes the basic unit of preparation in an academic programme;

12. The Credit: The basic unit for calculating the numerical value of the amount of work academically required to achieve the objectives of a given course;

13. The Curriculum: The set of courses of a programme.

14. The Programme: Defines the educational pathway and the system of evaluation and examinations for a specific educational level leading to a degree in a specified specialisation;

15. The Specialisation: A field of the domains of knowledge whose courses constitute the largest part of the courses of a programme leading to the holding of a degree bearing the name of this specialisation, in accordance with the standards describing the degree set forth in the regulations in force;

16. Quality Assurance: It is the set of measures designed to improve the performance of education and stimulate the raising of the level of all elements that affect higher education;

17. Accreditation: The recognition of a specific level of quality for the education provided; accreditation may be for an institution or for a programme;

18. Evaluation: The mechanism followed to examine an institution, a programme, or a group of programmes in an institution of higher education, in accordance with specified standards and indicators;

19. Self-Evaluation: The evaluation carried out by an institution of higher education through the internal quality assurance unit in accordance with specified mechanisms and standards;

20. External Evaluation: The evaluation undertaken by a specialised body independent of the Ministry and of the institution that subjects itself or one of its components thereto;

21. The Evaluation Body: An independent body whose mission is to evaluate private institutions of higher education or their programmes in accordance with specified mechanisms and standards.

Personality of Higher Education Institutions:

1. Lawfully established higher education institutions shall enjoy legal personality and administrative, financial, and academic autonomy within the rights guaranteed by the Constitution and the law;

2. Higher education institutions subject to the provisions of this law shall enjoy the inviolability of their campuses, and security forces may not enter such campuses except in execution of a judicial warrant or at the request of the president of the institution or the person acting in his stead.

Degrees awarded by institutions:

Private higher education institutions shall award the following degrees:

1. The Bachelor's degree (Licence), which is the first-level degree, and the duration of study required to obtain it is at least three academic years, or the equivalent thereof in semesters, provided that the duration of study in this case shall not be less than two and a half academic years, in addition to the total credits required to obtain it, in accordance with the system adopted at the institution and duly approved by the Council for Higher Education.

Persons holding the Secondary School Certificate shall enroll in the programmes leading to this degree;

2. The Master's degree (Master), which is the second-level degree, and the duration of study required to obtain it is at least two academic years after obtaining the first-level degree, in accordance with the system adopted at the institution and duly approved by the Council for Higher Education;

3. The Doctorate degree, which is the third-level degree, and the duration of study required to obtain it is at least three academic years after obtaining the second-level degree, in accordance with the system adopted by the institution and duly approved by the Council for Higher Education;

4. A university degree leading to regulated professions, the designation of which, the duration of study required to obtain it, and the conditions of study therefor from both the academic and professional aspects, shall be determined by a decree passed by the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the Minister, observing the conditions governing the practice of professions provided for by the laws and regulations in force.

The decree shall include special regulations relating to second-level and third-level degrees in this case;

5. The Specialized Technical Diploma degree, a degree issued by the Ministry of Education and Higher Education following a study of not less than two years in higher technical institutes after holding the Secondary School Certificate;

6. Professional Educational Degrees:

- The Bachelor's degree (Licence) in Education: subject to the Bachelor's degree (Licence) system mentioned in Clause (1) of this Article;

- The Teaching Diploma: which is a one-year degree following the university Bachelor's degree (Licence) and specializes in an educational or procedural subject adopted in the curricula, and the Teaching Diploma may be taught in parallel with the courses of the Bachelor's degree (Licence) such that the duration of study required to obtain the Teaching Diploma and the Bachelor's degree (Licence) shall not be less than four academic years or the equivalent thereof in semesters.

- The Master's degree in Education: subject to the Master's system mentioned in Clause (2) of this Article; the conditions of study in these degrees from both the academic and professional aspects, their specializations, their concentrations, and the general framework of the content of the programmes shall be determined by a decree passed by the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the Minister based on the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education;

7. The Lebanese Bachelor's degree (Licence) in Law: the Lebanese State alone shall have the right to award the Lebanese Bachelor's degree (Licence) in Law and to approve and supervise the curricula of studies and the examinations for this degree.

Composition of the Council of Higher Education:

A council named the Council of Higher Education shall be established, chaired by the Minister of Education and Higher Education and composed of Lebanese nationals as follows:

- The Director General of Higher Education;

- The President of the Lebanese University or whoever he delegates from among the deans at the University;

- An active judge from the State Council or a retired judge from its cadre;

- Three representatives of the private institutions of higher education;

- Two experts in higher education;

- The head of the syndicate or the heads of the syndicates competent in the subject matter, if the institution intended to be opened or the newly introduced programmes in a licensed institution prepare for granting degrees that entitle their holder to the right of affiliation to the concerned syndicate.

Appointment of the members of the Council of Higher Education:

1. The State Council shall assign to the membership of the Council of Higher Education a judge chosen from among its counsellor judges who have reached at least the tenth grade in its cadre, or shall appoint one of those who have retired from this cadre and have been granted honorary status;

2. Private institutions of higher education shall be represented as follows:

a. Two representatives of the universities that have practised higher education for at least 50 years,

b. One representative of the universities that have practised higher education for a period exceeding 15 years and less than 50 years. Each of the two groups of universities referred to above shall choose those who represent it by election, and under no circumstances may a person whose membership in the Council has expired be re-elected until a full term of office has elapsed from the date of such expiration, provided that rotation among the institutions is observed.

3. The Minister shall choose the two experts from among those who nominate themselves for this position, whereby each of them shall submit his candidacy in writing by a letter addressed to the Minister for this purpose;

4. Each of the representatives of the private institutions of higher education in the Council must be a president of the institution, a vice-president, or a dean in institutions where the position of vice-president is not available, and must have experience in university teaching of not less than fifteen years, in addition to proven experience in university administration or in quality systems for a period of at least five years;

5. The two experts must be of recognised academic and administrative experience in the field of higher education for a period of not less than 20 years, and must not be affiliated with any of the institutions of higher education operating in Lebanon, and they may be retirees from public and private institutions of higher education. The term of membership in the Council shall be set at three years for all those whose membership is not ex officio.

Enrolment in Higher Education Institutions:

The following shall be determined by decisions of the Minister upon the proposal of the Higher Education Council based on the opinion of the Academic Technical Committee:

1. The secondary certificate that permits the pursuit of study in the various types of higher education;

2. The conditions for transferring between the various types of higher education (academic, technological, technical);

3. The minimum conditions for the transfer of students between higher education institutions;

4. The conditions for holding more than one degree in more than one specialisation.

General Frameworks for Study in Higher Education:

The general frameworks for study in the levels, types and specialisations of higher education shall be determined by a decree adopted in the Council of Ministers;

upon the proposal of the Minister based on a recommendation from the Higher Education Council supported by the opinion of the Academic Technical Committee.

On the nomination and election of the representatives of the private institutions of higher education:

1. A private higher education institution nominating a representative thereof for membership of the Council shall satisfy the following conditions:

. That it has practised teaching continuously for a period of fifteen years and graduated at least seven cohorts of the first level and three cohorts of the second level;

. That none of the penalties of the first and second categories provided for in this Law has been imposed thereon in the last three years;

. That the number of full-time students registered therein is not less than one thousand five hundred students.

2. The right of a higher education institution to nominate or to vote in the elections of the Council, which shall take place after the expiry of six years from the formation of the first Council of Higher Education in accordance with the provisions of Article Fourteen of this Law, shall be conditional upon its having obtained institutional accreditation in accordance with what is provided for in this Law.

Functions of the Council of Higher Education:

The Council shall assume the following powers and functions in accordance with the provisions of this Law:

1. Contributing to the development of the national policy for higher education in general, and in particular that relating to private higher education;

2. Submitting proposals capable of spreading and regulating higher education within the framework of the national policy for higher education in the light of the Constitution and the laws in force;

3. Proposing the foundations and standards for establishing private institutions of higher education or branches thereof and for introducing programmes therein;

4. Submitting recommendations regarding applications for permission to establish;

5. Recommending the commencement of teaching;

6. Recommending the introduction of new programmes in existing faculties and institutes, provided that these programmes clearly fall within the fields of study covered by these faculties or institutes;

7. Approving the programmes and regulations and the amendments thereof submitted by private institutions of higher education which are subject to a decision by the Council pursuant to this Law and the decrees and regulations adopted in application thereof;

8. Examining the reports prepared by the National Authority for Quality Assurance regarding private institutions of higher education and their programmes and taking the appropriate decisions or recommendations based on these reports, as follows:

a. Recommending the revocation of the permission to establish institutions, branches or programmes due to their failure to fulfil the technical and academic conditions;

b. Taking decisions to consider a programme as recognised for a specified period of time;

9. Informing the National Authority for Quality Assurance of developments relating to the legal status of private institutions of higher education and their programmes;

10. Establishing committees and seeking the assistance of such experts as it deems appropriate to support it in carrying out functions outside the scope of the functions of the Academic Technical Committee, or tasking the latter therewith;

11. Expressing an opinion to the Minister on all matters concerning higher education;

12. Carrying out all other functions provided for in this Law and in the decrees and regulations adopted in application thereof.

The decisions and recommendations of the Council of Higher Education set forth in items 3 to 8 of this Article shall be based on the reports of the Academic Technical Committee and the committees specialised in the programmes.

Composition of the Academic Technical Committee:

1. The Academic Technical Committee shall be composed of eleven members by a decision of the Minister as follows: the Director General of Higher Education, as Chairman, a judge from the State Council, as a member, four experts from private higher education institutions, as members, three experts from the Lebanese University, as members, two experts not affiliated with any of the higher education institutions.

2. The State Council shall assign to the membership of the Committee a judge whom it selects from among the counsellor judges in its cadre, or it shall name one of those who have retired from this cadre and have been considered in an honorary capacity;

3. It shall be required that the expert who is a member of the Committee have experience of at least fifteen years in higher education, and have been classified in one of the two highest ranks of the ranks of members of the teaching staff, in addition to reliable experience in university administration or in quality systems for a period of at least three years;

4. The term of office of all experts on the Committee shall be fixed at six years, with the exception of the first body formed for it, as the membership of four of its members shall last for a period of three years only;

5. Upon the expiry of three years of the term of the first body formed for this Committee after the issuance of this Law, two experts from among those who were selected from the experts in private higher education institutions, as well as one of the two experts from the Lebanese University, and one of the two experts not affiliated with any higher education institution, shall be replaced by lot, while the remaining experts who were not drawn by lot shall continue in the membership of the Committee for three following years;

6. Upon the completion of the term of the first body, half the number of its expert members shall be renewed by replacing the four of them who have completed six years of their membership therein with substitutes for them for a full term, and this renewal mechanism shall be adopted periodically every three years.

Manner of selecting the members of the Academic Technical Committee:

1. Each of the private higher education institutions that fulfil the conditions provided for in Article 15 of this Law shall draw up a list of three experts, and the Lebanese University shall draw up a list of six experts upon the nomination of the University Council who meet the conditions provided for in Article 21, and shall submit it to the Council for Higher Education;

2. The Council for Higher Education shall examine the files of the expert professors proposed by the private higher education institutions and by the Lebanese University with respect to their fulfilment of the conditions, and it may also seek the assistance of experts from outside the Council to examine these files;

3. The Council shall draw up a list comprising twelve names from among the experts nominated by the private higher education institutions and four from among those nominated by the Lebanese University, and six names of experts not affiliated with any of the higher education institutions, and shall submit this list to the Minister, for him to select the members of the Committee from the names included therein and appoint them as members thereof in accordance with the rules of its formation, by a decision he takes for this purpose; the diversity of their specialisations and their distribution across the various educational systems and languages adopted in higher education institutions in Lebanon shall necessarily be observed in the selection of the experts.

Rules of meetings of the Academic Technical Committee:

1. The Committee shall meet every fifteen days, and shall also meet as necessary at the invitation of its Chairman or at the request of at least four of its members;

2. The Committee shall draw up its rules of procedure, which shall be approved by a decision of the Minister upon the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education.

Manner of Selecting the List of Members of the Committees Specialised in Programmes:

1. The Minister shall determine by a decision issued by him, upon the recommendation of the Council based on the proposal of the Academic Technical Committee, a list of the specialisations for which committees are required to be formed;

2. Private higher education institutions shall draw up a list of the specialists they have who meet the conditions specified above, including those whose services therein have ended due to reaching the retirement age;

3. The Academic Technical Committee shall examine the files of the specialists as to their fulfilment of the conditions specified above;

4. The Minister shall approve, upon the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education, the list of specialists who meet the conditions, and it shall be adopted and notified to all higher education institutions;

5. The Committees Specialised in Programmes shall be composed by a decision of the Minister upon the proposal of the Academic Technical Committee, according to need from among the names included in the adopted list, subject to observing the non-participation of an expert in examining a file of a higher education institution in which he is considered a member of its teaching or administrative staff, a member of one of its councils, or a partner in its ownership;

6. The distribution of its members across the various educational systems and languages adopted in higher education institutions in Lebanon shall necessarily be observed in the composition of each committee;

7. The formation of the list of specialists shall be reviewed every three years.

Formation of the Committee:

The Committee shall be formed by a decision of the Minister as follows:

1. The Director General of Higher Education, as Chairman;

2. A judge from the State Council;

3. Two experts from the Lebanese University;

4. Four experts from private higher education institutions;

The term of office of all experts on the Committee shall be fixed at six years, with the exception of the first body formed therefor, in which the membership of three of its expert members shall last for a period of three years only; upon the expiry of three years of the term of office of the first body formed for this Committee after the issuance of this Law, two of the experts chosen from among the experts in private higher education institutions, as well as one of the two experts from the Lebanese University, shall be replaced by lot, while the remaining experts who were not drawn by lot shall continue their membership in the Committee for the following three years;

Upon the completion of the term of office of the first body, half of its expert members shall be renewed by replacing the three of them whose membership therein has lasted six years with substitutes for them for a full term of office, and this renewal mechanism shall be adopted periodically every three years;

Rules and conditions for selecting the members of the Committee:

1. The Minister shall select the members of the Committee in accordance with the mechanism adopted for selecting the members of the Academic Technical Committee and from the list of experts adopted for selecting the members of the same Committee;

2. In selecting the experts, due regard shall mandatorily be given to the diversity of their specialisations and their distribution across the various educational systems and languages adopted in higher education institutions in Lebanon;

3. The State Council shall assign to the membership of the Committee a judge whom it selects from among the advisor judges on its cadre, or shall designate one of those who have retired from this cadre and have been placed in an honorary position;

Functions of the Committee:

The Committee shall adjudicate upon:

a. Whether a degree or certificate granted by higher education institutions operating outside Lebanon, of whatever type and level, fulfils the educational standard conditions prescribed by the laws, decrees and regulations in force in Lebanon; and its equivalence to the corresponding degrees or certificates in Lebanon;

b. Allowing a person who has completed years, credits or studies abroad that did not culminate in a university degree to pursue studies in Lebanon in a specified specialisation;

c. The rights and privileges enjoyed by holders of degrees or certificates that have no counterpart in the higher education system in Lebanon.

Rules of meetings of the Committee:

1. The Committee shall meet weekly at fixed times, and shall also meet as necessary upon convocation by its Chairperson or at the request of at least three of its members;

2. The Committee shall lay down its rules of procedure, which shall be approved by a decision of the Minister upon the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education.

Accreditation of Private Higher Education Institutions and Their Programmes:

1. The accreditation of private higher education institutions in Lebanon and their programmes aims to ensure the regularity of their work and their administrative and academic performance in delivering specialisations, programmes and degrees, in a manner that provides for and preserves the rights of students and ensures the quality of the academic process and its development and improvement, in conformity with the internationally recognised standards in higher education, taking into account the diversity in the higher education systems adopted in Lebanon;

2. Every private higher education institution must subject itself and its programmes, at its own expense, to self-evaluation and to external evaluation, with a view to obtaining institutional accreditation or accreditation for its programmes, in accordance with requirements determined by a decree issued by the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the Minister based on rules in this regard established by the National Commission for Quality Assurance.

Lebanese Moral Persons Entitled to Submit an Application A Lebanese moral person who may submit an application for a licence to establish a private higher education institution means each of the following persons of private law:

1. Civil companies and associations whose primary objectives include the dissemination of higher education and which do not seek profit;

2. Independent religious institutions specialised in education, for which the statute of the confession concerned recognises the capacity to acquire special rights and obligations enabling them to establish and manage a private higher education institution independently.

Conditions relating to the non-Lebanese legal person:

To submit an application for a licence for a private higher education institution, a non-Lebanese legal person must meet the following conditions:

1. That it is an operating higher education institution licensed in a state recognised by the Lebanese State and holding accreditation from a quality assurance and accreditation body recognised in Lebanon;

2. That this institution grants degrees recognised in the country to which it belongs;

3. That the higher education institution established in Lebanon is a branch of the parent institution, and that the latter undertakes to exercise actual supervision over the branch requested to be established;

4. That it undertakes to abide by the Lebanese laws and regulations governing higher education;

5. That it has a Lebanese representative who fulfils the conditions required to be met by the president of a higher education institution, who legally represents it in Lebanon before the official departments and the Lebanese courts in all cases, receives all notifications and correspondence addressed to it, and has a place of residence in Lebanon at the time of applying for the licence.

If the foreign legal person chooses to provide higher education in Lebanon in cooperation with a Lebanese legal person, it is required, in addition to the conditions specified in the first paragraph of this article, that the Lebanese party with whom it wishes to cooperate fulfils the conditions that entitle it to obtain a licence to establish a higher education institution in Lebanon.

Application documents:

The licence application shall be submitted in accordance with a special form approved by the Ministry and shall be accompanied by documents relating to:

1. The bylaws of the legal person applying for the licence;

2. The bylaws of the institution that is the subject of the licence application;

3. An undertaking to abide by the conditions set forth in the laws and regulations of higher education

4. The financial solvency of the institution.

Documents relating to the institution:

The documents relating to the higher education institution which is the subject of the application for an establishment licence shall be determined as follows:

First - The following management regulations of the institution:

1. The bylaws showing the university's mission, vision and objectives, and showing the independence of the institution's management from the management of the licence-requesting body in the conduct of its internal academic and administrative affairs;

2. The regulations and structure of the senior management that supervises the institution;

3. The regulations and structure of the academic management that determine the qualifications required for the appointment of the president of the institution, his deputies if any, the deans and all other academic officials, and the powers of each of them;

4. The internal regulations of the institution, its academic units and all its campuses;

5. Documentation mechanisms;

6. The staff regulations for non-teaching staff employed by it, provided that they include a provision reserving a proportion of at least ninety percent of them for Lebanese nationals, and provided that they are subject to the provisions of the Lebanese Labour Law;

7. Self-evaluation systems for the institution and its programmes.

Second - The financial regulations of the institution showing that a special budget of its own, independent of the budget of the legal person applying for the licence, shall be drawn up annually for it, and determining in particular:

1. The rules for approving and disbursing the budget;

2. The percentage thereof allocated to research in accordance with what is provided for in this Law;

3. The percentage allocated to financial aid for students, provided that it is not less than five percent of the total student tuition fees:

And there shall be attached to the application:

1. An estimated budget for the establishment and operation of the institution drawn up by one of the financial auditing firms classified by the Ministry of Finance;

2. Proof of the financial capacity to cover the expenses of establishment and then of operation for a period of three years, by means of a bank certificate issued by one of the banks in Lebanon, showing the availability of such capacity;

3. A bank guarantee equivalent to the operational value for a period of three years;

Third - A list of the faculties and institutes that will be established and affiliated with the institution, showing:

1. The location of each of them, the real property on which it will be erected, and the buildings erected or to be erected thereon and their absorptive capacity, by producing a real estate certificate proving the ownership of the real property by the applicant for the licence, or an official deed proving his right to erect the institution thereon, provided that the duration of the institution's holding of this right is at least twenty-five years.

It is required that the university campuses and centres be independent and not linked to economic, residential or non-university educational purposes or any other purposes that are not for higher education.

2. Detailed plans of the university buildings and facilities in accordance with a reference guide drawn up by the Technical Academic Committee for that purpose;

3. The specialisations intended to be taught in each faculty or institute and the degree to which the instruction will lead, and a statement of the following:

a. The educational programmes and curricula for each specialisation in accordance with the definitions set out in the first Article of this Law,

b. The equipment and teaching and learning resources including laboratories, studios, workshops and libraries.

Fourth - The special regulations for members of the teaching staff, showing the following:

1. The conditions for hourly contracting, full-time status and entry into the permanent cadre, where applicable;

2. The university qualifications and the academic and professional experience required to be held by a member of the teaching staff to teach each level of the degrees awarded by the institution;

3. The bases for classifying members of the teaching staff and the mechanism for their promotion in accordance with the minimum standards, which shall be determined by a decision of the Minister upon a recommendation of the Council based on a proposal of the Technical Academic Committee;

4. Social benefits for full-time professors or those in the permanent cadre.

5. Basic rights and duties including academic freedoms, intellectual property and grievance mechanisms.

Obligations which the licence applicant undertakes:

The licence applicant shall attach to the documents proving its legal entity a letter of undertaking authenticated by a notary public, whereby it commits to:

1. Refraining from:

A. Reassigning or leasing the licence to any party whatsoever,

B. Commencing instruction prior to obtaining authorization to do so,

C. Teaching any specialization that is not officially licensed;

D. Commencing teaching on any campus of the institution prior to obtaining the necessary licence,

E. The institution, after being licensed to be established, carrying out any act of whatever nature that would enable a foreign institution of higher education not licensed in Lebanon to practise or provide higher education therein;

2. Advertising the institution and the levels of education and specializations therein in accordance with the content of the licensing decree;

3. Adopting systems for the self-evaluation of the institution and its programmes;

4. The number of members of its teaching staff being proportionate to the total number of its students in accordance with criteria issued by a decision of the Minister upon the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education based on a proposal of the Academic Technical Committee;

5. Observing the ratio of doctorate holders and full-time members of the teaching staff in each educational programme in which the institution awards an academic degree in accordance with criteria issued by a decision of the Minister upon the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education based on a proposal of the Academic Technical Committee;

6. Appointing the persons in charge of its laboratories, studios, workshops, and libraries from among specialists and in accordance with the specific regulations of each faculty.

Establishment of a faculty or an institute:

The documents that a legally licensed and operating institution of higher education must attach to the licence application to establish a faculty affiliated to it or an institute subordinate to it shall be determined as follows:

1. The internal regulations of the faculty or institute sought to be established;

2. The documents mentioned in Paragraph "Third" of Article 44 of this Law;

3. The qualifications of the members of the teaching staff with whom it will contract to secure the tasks of instruction in the faculty or institute intended to be established;

4. An estimated budget for the establishment and operation of the faculty or institute, prepared by one of the financial auditing firms accredited by the Ministry of Finance;

5. Proof of financial capacity to cover the expenses of establishment and operation for a period of three years, by means of a bank certificate issued by one of the banks in Lebanon showing the availability of such capacity.

6. A bank guarantee equivalent to the operational value for a period of three years;

Establishment of an additional campus:

The documents that a legally licensed and operating higher education institution is required to attach to the licensing application, to establish an additional campus, shall be determined as follows:

1. The internal bylaws of the campus sought to be established and its administrative and academic relationship with the parent institution;

2. The documents mentioned in Paragraph "Third" of Article 44 of this Law;

3. The qualifications of the members of the teaching staff with whom it will engage to secure teaching duties at the campus intended to be established;

4. An estimated budget for establishing and operating the campus prepared by one of the financial auditing companies accredited by the Ministry of Finance;

5. Proof of financial capability to cover the expenses of establishment and operation for a period of three years, by means of a bank certificate issued by one of the banks in Lebanon, demonstrating the availability of this capability.

Establishment of a specialisation or a degree of the second or third level:

The documents required to be attached to the licensing application for an operating higher education institution to provide education in a specialisation or an educational level that it had not been licensed to teach, within a faculty or institute at an existing campus, shall be determined as follows:

1. The educational programmes and curricula in accordance with the definitions set forth in the First Article of this Law;

2. The equipment and means of teaching and learning, including laboratories, studios, workshops, and libraries, necessary for teaching the specialisation in which education is sought to be imparted;

3. The qualifications of the members of the teaching staff with whom it will engage to secure teaching duties in each of the subjects of the specialisation that is the subject of the licensing application;

4. Proof of financial capability to cover the expenses of equipping and operation for a period of three years, by means of a bank certificate issued by one of the banks in Lebanon, demonstrating the availability of this capability.

5. A bank guarantee equivalent to the operational value for a period of three years.

Conditions for establishing a Doctorate programme:

Upon submitting an application for a licence to establish a programme at the Doctorate level, the following conditions must be fulfilled in the institution:

1. That the required conditions for offering programmes leading to this degree be satisfied in accordance with the standards and procedures referred to in this Law and the regulations issued in implementation thereof, and that it have graduated ten annual cohorts of students from second-level programmes;

2. That it have a plan for the development of scientific research that provides for the human and material capacities to put it into effect;

3. That it provide for a special regulation on the conditions of admission and graduation for the Doctorate degree;

4. That the institution have the administrative, technical and human capacities capable of providing the requirements of the programme.

5. The special conditions for teaching Master's programmes shall be laid down by a decree in the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the Minister based on the recommendation of the Council for Higher Education. Joint Doctorate programmes between more than one higher education institution may be licensed, provided that each of them satisfies the conditions stipulated in this Article jointly.

Constituents of Public Order in Higher Education:

The following elements shall be considered among the constituents of public order in higher education with which a private higher education institution must comply:

1. Obtaining an establishment licence by a decree issued in the Council of Ministers;

2. Obtaining permission to commence instruction by a decision of the Minister;

3. Identifying itself by its name indicating the true nature of its type in accordance with the licence it has obtained;

4. Providing education exclusively in the specialisation or specialisations for which it is licensed in order to obtain the degrees it has been authorised to grant;

5. Complying with the regulations of the institution and securing the requirements of higher education in conformity with the stipulations of the legislative and regulatory texts governing its status.

6. Filing with the Directorate General of Higher Education an annual list of the names of the students enrolled therein in accordance with an electronic information schedule, the content and submission framework of which shall be determined by a decision of the Minister upon the proposal of the Academic Technical Committee.

Rules for the Management of the Institution:

1. The management of the private higher education institution shall be undertaken by a president and a council assisted by an administrative staff, and the president shall represent the institution vis-a-vis third parties;

2. The president shall be responsible for the institution's compliance with the laws, decrees, and regulations in force, and shall exercise the duties and powers determined for him by the internal bylaws of the institution;

3. Presidents of higher education institutions and of faculties affiliated with universities are required to hold a recognized doctorate degree and to fulfill the conditions for the rank of associate professor at least, or its equivalent.

4. Presidents of higher technical institutes are required to hold a recognized doctorate degree, or the highest degree in one of the specializations taught by the institute with experience in higher education of no less than five years.

Violation by the institution of the licensing conditions or its loss of one of them:

1. If the Council is notified that a higher education institution has violated or is violating any of the licensing conditions or that it has lost one of the conditions set forth in this Law or in the decrees and regulations issued in implementation thereof, or in any of the laws, decrees and regulations in force, it shall refer the matter to the Technical Academic Committee for verification and the expression of an opinion;

2. If the violation is established, the Minister shall address a formal notice to the administration of the institution requiring its removal within a time limit which he shall determine of not less than six months, and the Council for Higher Education may recommend to the Minister the extension of the time limit provided that the entire time limit does not exceed the end of the academic year immediately following that during which the violation is established;

3. If the administration of the institution does not remove the violation that has occurred within the specified time limit, the penalties provided for in this Law shall be applied to the institution.

General Objectives through Sustainable Water Management

The Ministry of Energy and Water shall undertake to establish a sustainable water policy with the objective of preserving the water wealth of the Lebanese State, and the public water establishments shall each, within the scope of its powers, undertake to provide water services across all aspects of their uses to users, and the powers of each of them have been defined in accordance with the provisions of Law No. 221/2000.

To this end, they must:

a - Give priority to supplying citizens with potable water;

b - Achieve sanitation for wastewater;

c - Combat floods, drought, and accidental or chronic pollution, and in general anticipate damages that may affect public health and safety and the methods of preventing them;

d - Meet the water needs required for agriculture, livestock breeding, hunting, fishing and pisciculture in inland waters, mineral water extraction, industry, energy generation, transport, tourism, the protection of aquatic and natural sites and landscapes, recreation, and all legally practised human activities;

e - Prevent any further deterioration and preserve and restore the aquatic environment, as well as the terrestrial ecosystems and wetlands linked thereto, with respect to their water needs;

f - Prevent pollution and work on reducing it gradually.

g - Benefit, wherever possible, from non-conventional water sources: water desalination, treated water resulting from wastewater treatment plants, and others.

Role of the Authority

The Authority shall have a consultative role and shall contribute to establishing a comprehensive definition of the general objectives and guiding principles for a general and sustainable national policy for the management of this sector, and the following matters shall be submitted to it:

1- The General Master Plan for Water and the basin plans;

2- Plans and programmes aimed at regulating water uses, preventing its waste and rationalising its consumption;

3- Projects and regulation of water distribution of national and regional importance;

4- The ability of the State to finance plans and projects, establish financing programmes and determine their sources;

5- Orientations and measures deemed appropriate by the competent ministries for the application of integrated water management, particularly in the field of protecting aquatic ecosystems, including tourism, industry, energy, forest management, agricultural activities, livestock breeding, fishing and urban planning.

6 - Determining the priorities of projects and their distribution across regions;

7- Preparing periodic reports on the activity of the Authority and the progress of its work.

Upon the proposal of the Minister of Energy and Water, the National Water Authority may formulate proposals concerning research, education, training and information in the field of water, with a view to improving the management of this resource.

The details of the composition, organisation and operation of the National Water Authority shall be determined by a decree adopted in the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the President of the Council of Ministers.

Contents of the General Master Plan for Water

The General Master Plan for Water in Lebanon shall include:

1- An analysis of the characteristics of water and aquatic ecosystems, which essentially determines the following:

أ - The determination of the quantities of water consumed and currently available, and those expected to become available, in the light of which the current and future water needs may be decided;

ب - The current water needs in the main sectors: human consumption, agriculture, livestock breeding, industries and similar activities, tourism and recreational activities (leisure)...

ج - The development of expected medium-term and long-term water needs in the various sectors of the State;

د - The state of water installations, sanitation systems and seawater desalination.

2- A statement comprising:

أ - The quantities of conventional and non-conventional, surface, ground and gushing (springs) water;

ب - The conditions of surface water and groundwater;

ج - The areas subject to special provisions;

د - The water conveyance areas planned for human supply.

3 - The objectives of sustainable water management, in particular:

أ - The quantitative objectives that must be achieved within a time period specified in the Master Plan, to meet the basic needs for water and the necessity of reconciling them with the surrounding environment and with the basic needs of the population;

ب - The qualitative objectives that allow reaching, within a time period specified by the Master Plan, an acceptable environmental state for surface, ground and coastal waters during which they become fit for human consumption;

ج - Securing the facilities and installations necessary to prevent the deterioration of water quality, provide protection therefor, and improve the state of water and aquatic ecosystems, and estimating the costs of this plan;

د - Determining the rules of the manner of informing citizens of the objectives and measures intended to be taken to maintain sustainable water management.

4 - The maps that allow the accurate designation of the elements mentioned in the Master Plan, in particular:

أ - Protected areas;

ب - Polluted areas and areas exposed to pollution risks;

ج - Water conveyance areas allocated for human consumption;

د - Irrigation areas;

ه - Areas exposed to a water deficit;

و - Areas where connection to the collective sanitation network is mandatorily required;

ز - Areas to which public institutions effectively provide potable water and collective sanitation;

ح - The main facilities intended to be established to meet the pressing need for water and the objectives of sustainable water management specified in the Master Plan, in particular facilities for extracting water, treating potable water, purifying wastewater, discharging treated and stored water, draining rainwater, or desalinating water;

The General Master Plan shall also designate the areas subject thereto in accordance with what is set out above.

Monitoring of Works and Activities

Installations, facilities, works and activities carried out by any natural or legal person belonging to either the public or private sector shall be subject to the monitoring regime where they result in:

1- The extraction of surface, ground, artesian or coastal waters and the return or non-return thereof to their source;

2- A modification in the level or in the mode of flow of waters;

3- Direct or indirect, chronic or circumstantial discharge, flow, disposal or sedimentation, even if it does not result in causing pollution in the aquatic ecosystem,

and results in affecting the waters or the aquatic ecosystems;

4- Determining the permitted depth for the extraction of groundwater.

Licensing Regime

Licences shall be issued by the Ministry of Energy and Water in accordance with the following provisions:

a - No individual, group, or natural or legal entity may establish any water facility to retain runoff water and water flowing in watercourses and valleys, or to divert it from its courses, except after obtaining a prior licence;

b - No water well may be deepened without obtaining a licence, under penalty of withdrawal of the previously granted licence and backfilling of the well at the expense of its owner;

c - No new well or replacement well may be drilled, nor may any water facility be established, unless the beneficiary obtains a valid licence for this purpose;

d - The beneficiary must comply with the conditions specified in the licence, which sets out the conditions necessary for execution;

e - It is prohibited to grant any licence for any works that may cause hazards relating to public health and safety, obstruct the flow of water, reduce water resources, significantly increase flood risks, or cause harm to the quality of the aquatic environment or to biodiversity in the aquatic medium;

f - All works dealing with water intended for non-domestic purposes shall be subject to prior licensing, and the same applies to irrigation facilities, hydroelectric facilities, fish farming, hydroponic installations, and water prospecting;

g - Subject to the application of the provisions of Decision No. 320 dated 26 May 1926, licences may be withdrawn or amended pursuant to a reasoned decision issued by the Minister of Energy and Water, without compensation, in the following cases:

1- If the decision is taken in the public interest;

2- To avert dangerous situations such as water scarcity or floods;

3- In the event of the emergence of a grave risk to the natural components of water and aquatic ecosystems;

4- When water facilities are abandoned and their operators no longer have need for them;

5- If the licensee fails to commence drilling works within the time limits specified in the licensing regime;

6- If he uses this licence for a purpose other than that for which it was granted;

7- If he violates the conditions contained in the licence;

8- If he assigns this licence to a third party for consideration or without consideration without the approval of the licensing authority;

9- The Ministry of Energy and Water is entitled to conduct a periodic review of granted licences and to withdraw such licences in the event of violation of the conditions on the basis of which the licence was granted.

General Report on the Conditions and Sustainable Management of Water

The Minister of Energy and Water shall prepare every year a general report addressing the conditions of the sustainable management of water.

The report shall include in particular:

1 - Measures for the implementation of this Law, whether in relation to decrees, decisions, circulars, plans, and programmes, or ordinary measures;

2 - The development of available quantitative and qualitative data;

3 - The outcome of the programme for the management and economic use of water;

4 - The measures taken by the Administration.

The annual report on the general situation and sustainable management of water shall be published in the Official Gazette, and a copy thereof shall be notified to the National Water Authority.

On Ascertaining Infringements

Infringements of the provisions of this Law and the decrees laid down for its implementation shall be established pursuant to infringement reports drawn up by judicial police officers, or employees of the Ministry of Energy and Water, or sworn staff members officially assigned thereto by the Public Water Exploitation Establishments within the scope defined for each establishment, in accordance with the laws in force.

To this end, they may perform the following:

1 - Enter the perimeter and the buildings of suspected installations, facilities, exploitations and establishments;

2 - Inspect all installations, facilities, machinery and warehouses;

3 - Obtain all documents relating to the installations and relevant activities;

4 - Take samples and take the necessary measures.

A settlement may be concluded regarding the infringement reports of committed infringements; drawn-up infringement reports must be referred immediately to the competent Public Prosecution through the Minister of Energy and Water upon the proposal of the Public Water Exploitation Establishments.

The competent court may also rule:

1 - To suspend operations, activities or works;

2 - To halt operations or prohibit the use of installations or facilities;

3 - To remove installations or facilities;

4 - To impose the rehabilitation of the aquatic environment or the ecosystem;

5 - To order the publication of its decision in full or excerpts thereof in two daily newspapers.

Every transporter of hazardous waste must submit an application to obtain an environmental licence in accordance with the form adopted at the Ministry of Environment.

A technical report showing at least the following shall be attached to the application:

The types of hazardous waste that will be transported;

The types and specifications of the vehicles approved for transporting hazardous waste and their parking locations;

The service areas and expected routes of the means of transport;

A detailed technical explanation of the transport operation including the necessary precautionary and preventive measures, the emergency plan and the training programme necessary to prevent the leakage of hazardous waste during the transport operation, and a statement of how to deal with unforeseen circumstances and accidents that may occur during the transport operation in a manner that ensures the protection of the environment;

A copy of an insurance policy valid throughout the entire period of the environmental licence

Provided that the technical report is prepared by one of the specialised offices accredited by the Ministry of Environment in accordance with instructions issued by the Minister pursuant to a decision determining the form and content of the technical report and the administrative procedures and time limits for reviewing it and expressing an opinion thereon.

The hazardous waste transporter shall comply with the environmental conditions specified in the environmental licence and with the following:

1 - Complying with the content and components of the technical report provided for in Article (19) of this Decree;

2 - Not transporting any hazardous waste that is not accompanied by the hazardous waste movement form approved by the Ministry of Environment, provided that this form accompanies the shipment at all stages of its transit;

3 - Not transporting hazardous waste unless it is packaged in the manner provided for in Article (14), Item (1) of this Decree;

4 - Not storing hazardous waste at the transporter's premises except where the transporter owns a storage facility environmentally licensed by the Ministry of Environment;

5 - Ensuring that the hazardous waste shipment is not delivered to any storage, recovery, treatment or final disposal facility that is not environmentally licensed by the Ministry of Environment;

6 - Ensuring the conformity of the shipment with the data recorded in the hazardous waste movement form before commencing the transport operation;

7 - Bearing liability for any damage or alteration occurring to the state of the hazardous waste during the transport operation and finding appropriate means to manage it in an environmentally sound manner in accordance with the environmental conditions specified in his environmental licence and in accordance with the laws and regulations in force;

8 - Officially notifying the Ministry of Environment within a period of 3 days of every accident that resulted or may result in an adverse impact on the environment during the transport of the hazardous waste;

9 - Returning the hazardous waste immediately to the waste generator in the event that it is not accepted upon delivery by the storage, recovery, treatment or final disposal facility;

10 - Submitting all transport forms to the Ministry of Environment upon its request therefor, provided that the Ministry of Environment specifies in its request the maximum period for submitting these documents;

11 - Providing the Ministry of Environment with periodic reports every three months on the various transport operations carried out by him, provided that the report includes copies of all completed forms relating to the movement of hazardous waste and accidents in the event of their occurrence, and provided that he retains copies of these reports for a period of five years from the date of their submission.

The Ministry of Environment shall have the right to impose new environmental conditions when necessary and to request documents proving the implementation of these new conditions.

A hazardous waste storage facility shall abide by the environmental management plan, the environmental conditions specified in the environmental permit, and the following:

1 - Refusal to receive any hazardous waste from a carrier that does not hold an environmental permit as a hazardous waste carrier;

2 - Refusal to accept the storage of any hazardous waste that is not accompanied by a hazardous waste movement form;

3 - Upon requesting the transport of any shipment of hazardous waste, ensuring that the hazardous waste movement form is completed and making sure that this form accompanies the shipment throughout all stages of its transit;

4 - Refusal to accept the receipt of hazardous waste unless it is packaged in the manner provided for in Article (14), Clause (1) of this Decree;

5 - Ensuring that the shipment of hazardous waste is not delivered or sent to any carrier or to any recovery, treatment, or final disposal facility that has not obtained an environmental permit from the Ministry of Environment;

6 - Matching the shipment against the data recorded in the hazardous waste movement form prior to commencing the storage process;

7 - Bearing liability for any damage or alteration occurring to the condition of the hazardous waste during the storage process, and finding appropriate means to manage it in an environmentally sound manner in accordance with the environmental conditions specified in its environmental permit and in accordance with the laws and regulations in force;

8 - Returning the hazardous waste immediately to its generator and at the latter's expense in the event that it is not accepted upon delivery for failing to meet the conditions;

9 - Notifying the Ministry of Environment within a period of 3 days of every incident that has resulted or may result in an adverse impact on the environment arising from the improper storage of hazardous waste;

10 - Providing the Ministry of Environment with periodic reports every three months on the various storage operations carried out by it, provided that the report includes the types and quantities of hazardous waste, the storage periods, incidents if any have occurred, the procedures and results of the monitoring process, and copies of all completed hazardous waste movement forms, and provided that copies of these reports are retained for a period of five years from the date of their submission.

The Ministry of Environment shall have the right to impose new environmental conditions when necessary and to request documents proving the implementation of these new conditions..

Hazardous waste recovery and/or treatment and/or final disposal facilities shall comply with the environmental management plan, the environmental conditions specified in the environmental licence, and the following:

1 - Not receiving any hazardous waste from a transporter who does not hold an environmental licence as a hazardous waste transporter;

2 - Not receiving any hazardous waste that is not accompanied by a hazardous waste movement form;

3 - Upon receiving any shipment of hazardous waste, verifying that the hazardous waste movement form has accompanied the shipment throughout all stages of its transit;

4 - Not receiving hazardous waste unless it is packaged in the manner stipulated in Article (14), Clause (1) of this Decree;

5 - Matching the shipment against the data recorded in the hazardous waste movement form prior to receiving it;

6 - Immediately returning the hazardous waste to its source (the generator or the storage facility) at the expense of its owner in the event that it is not accepted due to its failure to meet the conditions;

7 - Immediately notifying the Ministry of Environment of every accident resulting from improper recovery, treatment, or disposal of hazardous waste;

8 - Providing the Ministry of Environment with periodic reports every three months on the various recovery and/or treatment and/or final disposal operations carried out by them, provided that the report includes the types and quantities of hazardous waste, accidents in the event of their occurrence, the procedures and results of the monitoring process, and copies of all completed hazardous waste movement forms, provided that copies of these reports are retained for a period of five years from the date of their submission.

The Ministry of Environment shall have the right to impose new environmental conditions when necessary and to request documents proving the implementation of these new conditions.

The management of funds, depending on the stage of recovery of the funds, shall include exercising the right to invest the funds under recovery, that is at the stage of freezing or seizure, or disposing of the funds derived from the offences specified in this Law and all other offences, and the related proceeds that have been recovered by a final and binding judicial judgment, through regulations and a mechanism established for this purpose, until these funds are disbursed:

First: To contribute to covering its expenses and the expenses of the "Funds Recovery Department" and the "National Anti-Corruption Commission", and to rewarding, compensating and protecting whistleblowers of corruption;

And second: To provide the surplus of these funds, in the form of grants, to State projects aimed at combating poverty and achieving sustainable development.

Conditions for the participation of bidders:

First: Conditions of participation:

1. Bidders must satisfy the following conditions, in addition to any conditions deemed by the procuring entity to be appropriate and relevant to the subject matter of the procurement:

أ - That they have not been proven to have violated the professional ethics provided for in the relevant texts, if any;

ب - Legal capacity to conclude the procurement contract;

ج - Fulfilment of tax obligations and social security contributions;

Related text: Defining the concept of fulfilment of tax obligations for the application of the Public Procurement Law

د - That no final, even if not irrevocable, judgments have been rendered against them or against their managers or employees concerned with the procurement process convicting them of committing any offence relating to their professional conduct, or of submitting false or fabricated statements regarding their eligibility to conclude the procurement contract, or of corrupting a public procurement project or a tendering process, and that their eligibility has not otherwise been forfeited pursuant to administrative suspension or debarment proceedings, and that they are not in a state of exclusion from participating in public procurement;

ه - That they are not under liquidation or the subject of bankruptcy judgments rendered against them;

و - That they have not been convicted of the offences of habitual usury and money laundering pursuant to a final, even if not irrevocable, judgment;

ز- That they do not participate in the decision-making authority of the contracting authority and that they have no material interests or conflict of interest with any of the members of the decision-making authority;

ح - Other conditions imposed by the contracting authority in the special specifications for the procurement project which are proportionate to the required works.

ط -- A certificate from the Ministry of Economy and Trade proving compliance with the provisions of the Law on the Boycott of the Israeli Enemy in respect of foreign companies.

ي -- Declaration of the beneficial owners up to the ultimate degree of ownership.

2. Proof of the cessation of the impediment or rehabilitation shall automatically restore to the bidders the right to participate.

Second: Qualifications of bidders:

1. The qualifications of bidders mean the professional, technical and environmental qualifications, professional competence, financial resources, equipment and other physical facilities, managerial capability, experience and human resources to perform the contract as specified in the pre-qualification file or the tendering file, provided that they are determined in a manner proportionate to the subject matter of the procurement.

2. The procuring entity shall not impose with respect to the qualifications for the participation of bidders any criterion, condition or procedure that constitutes discrimination against them or among them or against categories of them, which cannot be objectively justified.

3. The procuring entity shall evaluate the qualifications for the participation of bidders in accordance with the qualification criteria and procedures set out in the tendering files.

4. The procuring entity shall disqualify any bidder in the following cases:

أ - If it establishes at any time that the information submitted concerning its qualifications is false or inaccurate or that it involves a material error or omission;

ب - If the qualified bidder fails to re-establish the availability of the qualifications on the basis of which pre-qualification took place in accordance with Article 19 of this Law.

Record of procurement proceedings:

1. The procuring entity shall establish a special record in which it shall include all information relating to the procurement process, and in which it shall keep all documents relating to the tendering proceedings and prepare and maintain files therefor. This record may be inspected in accordance with the provisions of this Article and shall be adopted as a reference that is easy to consult and obtain information from, and it shall be kept electronically where its availability is possible, and the following information shall be entered therein:

a - A brief description of the subject-matter of the procurement including mandatorily the dates of launching the procurement process, submitting tenders and applications to pre-qualify, if any, opening tenders and determining the winning tender;

b - The names and addresses of the tenderers and the name and address of the contracted entity, in addition to the value of the contract (and in the event of concluding a framework agreement: the name and address of the entity with which this agreement is concluded);

c - A statement of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity in taking its decision regarding the means of advertisement;

d - Where the procuring entity uses a procurement method other than public tender, a statement of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by it to justify the use of that other method;

e - In the event of conducting a framework agreement, a statement of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity to justify the use of framework agreement procedures;

f - In the event of cancellation of the procurement pursuant to paragraphs 1 and 2 of Article 25 of this Law, a statement to that effect and of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity in taking its decision to cancel the procurement;

g - In the event of applying socio-economic policies in the tendering proceedings, specifying detailed information on these policies and the mechanisms for activating them;

h - In the event of non-application of a standstill period according to paragraph 3 of Article 24 of this Law, a statement of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity to take its decision not to apply the standstill period;

i - In the event of challenge or review pursuant to the provisions of Chapter Seven of this Law, explicit reference must be made to all applications for reconsideration, complaints or reviews, and to the date and number of their registration in addition to a copy of the final decisions taken regarding each of them and reference to any withdrawal of these challenges and the reasons that led thereto;

j - A copy of the notice of the standstill period granted in accordance with paragraph 2 of Article 24 of this Law, where applicable;

k - Where the tendering proceedings have resulted in the award of the procurement pursuant to paragraph 8 of Article 24 of this Law, a statement to that effect and of the reasons that led thereto;

l - The value of the contract and its most important terms and conditions; in addition to a copy thereof. In the case of framework agreement procedures, a summary of its most important terms and conditions and a copy of the concluded written framework agreement shall be added;

m - The value of each tender submitted and a summary of its other most important terms and conditions;

n - Where the information is considered confidential pursuant to paragraph 1 of Article 6 or pursuant to Article 108 of this Law, specifying the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity in adopting confidentiality;

o - Any other information required to be entered in the record pursuant to the provisions of this Law or the laws in force or pursuant to the tendering documents;

p - In the event of rejection of a tender submitted pursuant to Article 27 of this Law, a statement to that effect and of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity in that decision;

q - A summary of the requests for clarification relating to the pre-qualification documents, if any, or the tendering files, and the responses to these requests, and a summary of every modification to these documents or files;

r - Information relating to the qualifications of tenderers submitting applications to pre-qualify and tenders, if any, or to their lack of qualifications;

s - In the event of exclusion of a tenderer from the tendering proceedings pursuant to Articles 7 or 8 of this Law, a statement to that effect and of the reasons and circumstances relied upon by the procuring entity in that decision.

2. The part of the record referred to in sub-paragraphs (a) to (i) of paragraph 1 of this Article shall be made available for inspection to any person after the provisional award or after the cancellation of the procurement, upon request.

3. Subject to the provisions of paragraph 4 of this Article, the part of the record referred to in sub-paragraphs (j) to (o) of paragraph 1 of this Article shall be made available for inspection, upon request, to tenderers who submitted tenders, after they have taken cognizance of the provisional award decision.

4. The provisions of Article 6 of this Law shall apply with respect to the information contained in the record, especially in relation to information connected with the opening and evaluation of submitted tenders, with the exception of the summary referred to in sub-paragraph (m) of paragraph 1 of this Article.

Drawing Up Procurement Plans:

1. The provisions of this Article shall apply to procurement projects whose estimated value exceeds a financial threshold set at /10/ billion Lebanese Pounds. This financial threshold shall be amended upon a recommendation from the Public Procurement Authority and pursuant to a decree adopted by the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of its President, provided that the amendment does not result in violating the objectives of this Article and the principles enshrined by this Law. Procurement operations of a confidential nature and relating to national security and defence pursuant to the fourth paragraph of Article 46 shall be exempted from the provisions of this Article.

2. The procuring entity shall determine its needs and prepare its annual plan for the upcoming year concurrently with the preparation of its public expenditures in accordance with the appropriations requested in its draft budget. The annual procurement plan shall be prepared on the basis of a standard template and procedures issued by the Public Procurement Authority. The plan may be annual or multi-year if it includes projects whose execution requires scheduling commitments within a medium- or long-term framework, provided that it includes at least the following information:

a - The subject of the procurement;

b - The nature of the procurement (goods, works, or services);

c - The source of funding and the estimated value by bracket, when it is possible to announce it, and its allocation in the budget;

d - A brief description of the procurement project;

e - The procurement method;

f - The probable date, indicating the month, with respect to the commencement of the awarding procedures;

g - The bracket within which the estimated value of the subject of the procurement falls;

h - Remarks and other useful information that would assist potential bidders in understanding and preparing sound bids within the time limits.

3 - The procuring entity shall send its completed plan to the Public Procurement Authority within a time limit of two months from the beginning of the fiscal year. The Public Procurement Authority shall consolidate the plans into a unified annual procurement plan and publish it within a time limit of 30 working days. The security and military forces shall be exempted from complying with the provisions of this paragraph instead of:

The procuring entity shall send its completed plan to the Public Procurement Authority within a time limit of ten working days from the date of adoption of the budget.

4 - Upon introducing any amendment to its annual plan, particularly obtaining loans or grants, the procuring entity must publish this amendment on its website, if any, and on the central electronic platform of the Public Procurement Authority, while observing the announcement time limits provided for in Article 12

