Evaluation criteria:

1. The evaluation criteria must be related to the subject matter of the procurement.

2. In addition to the price, the evaluation criteria may include the costs of operating, maintaining and repairing the goods or works, the time for delivery of the goods, completion of the works or provision of the services, the characteristics of the subject matter of the procurement, such as the functional characteristics of the goods or works and the environmental characteristics, the implementation plan and the experience of the work team, the terms relating to payment of the price, the guarantees related thereto and other criteria.

3. The evaluation criteria may encompass any criteria the taking into account of which is permitted under the laws in force or stipulated by the decrees or regulations in effect.

4. The procuring entity shall specify in the bidding documents:

(a) - the criteria that will be adopted for evaluation and whether it will be conducted on the basis of the lowest price or the best bid, which relies on criteria other than price;

(b) - all evaluation criteria established pursuant to this Article, including the price adjusted according to the margin of preference provided for in Article 16 of this Law or any form of preference;

(c) - the relative weights assigned to each of the evaluation criteria, provided that they are proportionate to the importance of the criterion and its need for the subject matter of the procurement;

(d) - the manner in which those criteria are to be applied in the evaluation proceedings.

5. In evaluating the submitted bids and determining the winning bid, the procuring entity shall use only the criteria and procedures set out in the bidding documents, and shall apply those criteria and procedures in the manner disclosed in those documents. No criterion or procedure not provided for in this paragraph shall be used.

Prequalification proceedings for bidders:

1. The procuring entity may conduct prequalification proceedings for bidders, in accordance with the provisions of this Law, in order to identify qualified bidders. The provisions of Article 7 of this Law shall apply to prequalification proceedings.

2. The invitation to prequalify shall be published in accordance with Article 12 of this Law.

3. The invitation to prequalify shall include the following:

(a) - The name and address of the procuring entity.

(b) - A summary of the principal terms and conditions required in the procurement contract or the framework agreement to be concluded in the course of the tendering proceedings, including the nature and quantity of the goods to be supplied and the place of their delivery, or the nature and location of the works to be executed, or the nature of the services and the location where they are to be provided, as well as the date of delivery of the goods, completion of the works, or provision of the services;

(c) - The criteria and procedures to be used to ascertain the qualifications of bidders, in accordance with Article 7 of this Law.

These criteria shall be subject to the prior approval of the Public Procurement Authority".

(d) - - The manner of obtaining the prequalification files and the place where such files may be received or inspected;

(e) - The manner and place of submission of applications to prequalify and the deadline for their submission, as well as the manner and place of submission of bids and the deadline for their submission, if already known at that time, in accordance with Article 20 of this Law;

(f) - The place and time (specified by hour, day, month and year) for opening the prequalification files;

(g) - The time limits within which bidders must be notified of the prequalification results;

(h) - The rules for notifying bidders of the prequalification results;

(i) - All additional information that the procuring entity decides to include in the invitation.

4. The procuring entity shall provide a set of prequalification documents on its website, if any, and on the central electronic platform of the Public Procurement Authority concurrently with the publication of the invitation.

5. The prequalification documents shall include the following information:

(a) - Instructions for preparing and submitting applications to prequalify;

(b) - Any evidence, documents or other information that must be submitted by bidders to demonstrate their qualifications;

(c) - The name of one or more officers or employees of the procuring entity who shall be responsible for communicating directly with bidders and receiving direct communications from them in connection with the prequalification proceedings, as well as the functional title and address of such officer or employee;

(d) - Cross-references to this Law and its implementing decrees, and all other laws directly related to the prequalification proceedings and their implementing decrees;

(e) - Any other conditions that may be established by the procuring entity in accordance with this Law regarding the preparation and submission of applications to prequalify and regarding the prequalification proceedings, provided that such additional conditions do not restrict the participation of potential bidders.

6. The procuring entity shall examine the qualification files of each bidder submitting an application to prequalify, and shall decide to qualify the bidders who meet the required conditions. In reaching its decision, the procuring entity shall apply only the criteria and procedures set forth in the invitation to prequalify and in the prequalification documents.

7. Only bidders who have been prequalified shall be entitled to continue to participate in the tendering proceedings, provided that the period of time separating the date of announcement of the prequalification results and the commencement of the tendering proceedings does not exceed a reasonable time limit commensurate with the importance and complexity of the project.

8. The procuring entity shall notify each bidder who submitted an application to prequalify of the result of their qualification, whether negative or positive.

9. The procuring entity shall notify each bidder who was not qualified of the reasons for their non-qualification.

10 - The Public Procurement Authority, in conjunction with the concerned entities, shall establish classification criteria for similar transactions and issue them by a regulatory decision.

Rules regarding Abnormally Low-Priced Tenders:

1. The procuring entity may reject any tender if it determines that the price, combined with the other constituent elements of that submitted tender, is abnormally low in relation to the subject matter of the procurement and its estimated value, and that it raises doubts on its part regarding the tenderer's ability to perform the contract, provided that the procuring entity has requested in writing from the concerned tenderer details of the submitted tender in a manner that allows it to analyse the detailed information and conclude whether the tenderer will be able to perform the procurement contract at the submitted price. Among the details that the procuring entity may request, by way of example and not limitation:

a - Information and samples or the like, proving the quality of the subject matter of the procurement submitted in the tender;

b - The relevant manufacturing methods;

c - The selected technical solutions and/or any exceptionally favourable conditions available to the tenderer for the performance of the contract that is the subject of the procurement.

2. The decision of the procuring entity to reject a tender in accordance with the provisions of this Article, the reasons for that decision, and all clarifications that took place with the tenderers shall be included in the evaluation report. The concerned tenderer shall be notified immediately of the decision of the procuring entity and the reasons therefor.

Contract value and conditions for its modification:

1- The consideration agreed upon in the contract shall be fixed and shall not be subject to modification and revision except when permitted during its performance within specified controls in accordance with the conditions for modification and revision in the following exceptional cases, provided that they are explicitly stipulated in the tender documents:

a- In application of formulas based on official local and, where necessary, international price indices when such formulas are not covered within the contract value;

b- In application of tax amendments leading to an increase in the cost of performing the contract;

c- When the need arises for additional quantities of works, goods, equipment, technology or services from the same supplier or contractor, for reasons relating to the standardisation of specifications or due to the need for compatibility with existing goods, equipment, technology, services or works, taking into account the effectiveness of the original procurement process in meeting the needs of the procuring entity, and provided that the value of the addition does not exceed 20% of the value of the original contract for supply and service contracts and 15% for works contracts;

d- In the case provided for in Paragraph 3 of Article 46;

e- When laws or decrees are issued that are capable of affecting the contract value, provided that this is justified pursuant to a report by the procuring entity.

2. The publication conditions provided for in Article 26 of this Law shall be observed when modifying the contract value.

Conditions for contracting by mutual agreement:

The procuring entity may procure by means of a mutual agreement, in accordance with the provisions of Section Seven of this Chapter, in the following exceptional circumstances:

1- Where the subject-matter of the procurement is available only from a single supplier or contractor, or where a supplier or contractor holds intellectual property rights in respect of the subject-matter of the procurement, and it is impossible to resort to another option or alternative;

2- In cases of emergency and relief resulting from the occurrence of a catastrophic and unforeseen event, as a consequence of which the use of any other procurement method is not a practical means of addressing such cases;

3- Where the procuring entity needs to contract with the original contractor when the following conditions are met cumulatively:

أ- The need arises during the performance of the contract;

ب- A state of extreme urgency exists and contracting is necessary in order to prevent delay in performance;

ج- It is necessary to standardise specifications and ensure compatibility or uniformity with the existing goods, equipment, technology, services or works;

د- The additions do not result in altering the objective of the original contract, upsetting its economics, or undermining the principle of competition;

ه- The supplies, works or services constitute an addendum to the original procurement and an integral part thereof, or the works must be executed at the work site;

و- The additional need could not have been foreseen at the time of the original contracting.

4- When procuring supplies or services or when executing works the confidential nature of which must be maintained for the requirements of national security or defence, pursuant to a decision taken in the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of the competent Minister defining the confidential nature of the procurement and the reasons for contracting by mutual agreement;

5- When contracting with persons of public law, such as public institutions and municipalities, or international organisations.

6- When contracting with hospitals, medical centres and laboratories.

Contents of the invitation to public tender:

The invitation to submit bids shall include the following information:

a- The name and address of the procuring entity;

b- A summary of the most important provisions and conditions required in the procurement contract, including the nature and quantity of the goods to be supplied and the place of their delivery, or the nature and location of the works to be executed, or the nature of the services and the location where they are to be provided, as well as the desired time or the time within which the goods must be supplied, the works completed, or the services provided;

c- The basis adopted for conducting the tender;

d- A summary of the criteria and procedures used to verify the qualifications of bidders, and of any documentary evidence or other information that bidders must submit to prove their qualifications, in accordance with Article 7 of this Law;

e- The value of the bid security, if applicable;

f- The place and manner of obtaining the bidding documents;

g- The place and time for inspecting the bidding documents;

h- The fee charged by the procuring entity for the bidding documents, the means of payment, and the currency in which it is paid, if there is a fee for them;

i- The language (Arabic) or languages in which the bidding documents are available;

j  The entity with which bids are deposited or to which they are addressed;

k- The procedures for submitting bids, and the place and deadline for their submission by hour, day, month, and year;

l- The place and time for opening the bids, provided that the time is specified by hour, day, month, and year;

m- All additional data and information that the procuring entity decides to include in the announcement.

Contents of the Books of Conditions (or Solicitation Documents):

The books of conditions shall include the following information:

(a) Instructions for preparing tenders;

(b) The criteria and procedures that shall be applied, in accordance with the provisions of Article 7 of this Law, to ascertain the qualifications of tenderers and any additional evidence regarding such qualifications;

(c) The requirements relating to the documentary evidence of the availability of qualifications or other information that must be submitted by tenderers to prove their qualifications;

(d) A detailed description of the subject-matter of the procurement, in accordance with Article 17 of this Law, the quantity of the goods to be procured, the services to be provided, the location where the goods are to be delivered, the works executed or the services provided, and the required time for supplying the goods, completing the works or providing the services, if any;

(e) The terms and conditions of the contract and the contract form to be signed by both parties, if any;

(f) Where alternatives to the characteristics of the subject-matter of the procurement or to the terms and conditions or to other requirements set forth in the solicitation documents are permitted, a statement to that effect and a description of the manner in which alternative tenders are to be evaluated;

(g) Where tenderers are permitted to submit tenders for only a portion of the subject-matter of the procurement, a specification and description of the portion or portions for which tenders may be submitted;

(h) The manner in which the tender price is to be formulated and expressed, including a statement as to whether the price includes elements other than the cost of the subject-matter of the procurement itself, such as including any applicable customs duties and taxes;

(i) The currency or currencies in which the tender price is to be formulated and expressed;

(j) The language (Arabic) or languages in which tenders are to be prepared, in accordance with Article 4 of this Law;

(k) Any requirements of the procuring entity with respect to the issuer of any tender security to be provided by the tenderer in accordance with Articles 34 and 35 of this Law, and with respect to the nature, form, amount and other principal terms and conditions of such security, and any such requirements for any security for the performance of the procurement contract to be provided by the supplier or contractor that enters into the procurement contract, including securities such as guarantees relating to labour and equipment;

(l) The manner, place and deadline for the submission of tenders, in accordance with the provisions of Article 20 of this Law;

(m) The means by which tenderers may seek clarifications of the solicitation documents pursuant to Article 21 of this Law, and a statement as to whether the procuring entity intends, at this stage, to convene a meeting of tenderers;

(n) The period of time during which tenders shall be valid, in accordance with Article 22 of this Law;

(o) The manner, place and date, by hour, day, month and year, for the opening of tenders, in accordance with Article 54 of this Law;

(p) The criteria and procedures for the evaluation of tenders based on the description of the subject-matter of the procurement and in accordance with Articles 18 and 55 of this Law;

(q) The currency that will be used for evaluating tenders;

(r) References to this Law and its implementing decrees, if any, and other laws and decrees directly pertinent to the procurement proceedings, including those applicable to procurement involving classified information;

(s) The name, functional title and address of one or more officers or employees of the procuring entity who are authorized to communicate directly with and to receive communications directly from tenderers in connection with the procurement proceedings;

(t) Notice of the right provided under Chapter VII of this Law to complaint or review against decisions or actions taken by the procuring entity that are allegedly non-compliant with the provisions of this Law, together with information on the duration of the applicable standstill period and, where no standstill period applies, a statement to that effect and the reasons therefor;

(u) Any formalities that will be required once a winning submitted tender has been accepted for a procurement contract to enter into force, pursuant to Article 24 of this Law;

(v) Any additional requirements established by the procuring entity, in conformity with this Law and its implementing decrees.

Request for proposals procedures for consulting services:

1- Where the procuring entity conducts procurement by means of a request for proposals in accordance with the provisions of Article 45, the invitation to participate shall be made in accordance with Article 12 of this Law.

2- The invitation shall include the following:

a- The name and address of the procuring entity;

b- A detailed description of the subject matter of the procurement in accordance with Article 17 of this Law, in addition to the date and place of delivery;

c- The terms and conditions of the procurement contract and the contract form to be signed by the two parties, if any;

d- The criteria and procedures to be used for ascertaining the qualifications of bidders and any documentary evidence or other information that must be submitted by bidders to demonstrate their qualifications;

e- The criteria and procedures for opening and evaluating proposals in accordance with Articles 15 and 18 of this Law, including the minimum requirements regarding the technical, functional and performance characteristics that proposals must meet in order to be considered responsive to the requirements in accordance with Article 17 of this Law, and a statement to the effect that proposals that do not meet those requirements shall be rejected as non-responsive to the requirements;

f- The declaration required by Article 12 of this Law;

g- The means of obtaining the request for proposals and the place from which it may be obtained;

h- The price charged by the procuring entity for the request for proposals, if it has a price;

i- The means of payment of the price of the request for proposals and the currency in which it is to be paid, if charged;

j- The language (Arabic) or languages in which the requests for proposals are available;

k- The manner of submitting proposals and the place and deadline for submitting them.

3- The procuring entity shall provide the request for proposals via its website, if any, and on the electronic central platform at the Public Procurement Authority, in addition to:

a- Every bidder responding to the invitation to participate in the request for proposals procedures, in accordance with the procedures and requirements specified therein; or

b- Every bidder pre-qualified in accordance with Article 19 of this Law, in the case of pre-qualification.

4- The request for proposals shall include, in addition to the information referred to in sub-paragraphs (a) to (e) and (k) of paragraph 2 of this Article, the following information:

a- Instructions for preparing and submitting proposals, including instructions directed to bidders to submit proposals to the procuring entity simultaneously in two envelopes: one containing the technical, functional and performance characteristics of the proposal, and the other containing the financial aspects of the proposal;

b- A description of the lot or lots for which proposals may be submitted, where bidders are permitted to submit proposals for only a lot of the subject matter of the procurement;

c- The currency or currencies in which the proposal price is to be formulated and expressed;

d- The manner in which the proposal price is to be set and expressed, including a statement as to whether the price is to include elements other than the cost of the subject matter of the procurement itself, such as customs duties or taxes;

e- The means by which bidders may request clarification regarding the request for proposals in accordance with the provisions of Article 21 of this Law, and a statement as to whether the procuring entity intends to convene a meeting of bidders at this stage;

f- Cross-references to this Law and to its implementing decrees and other laws directly related to the procurement proceedings;

g- The name of one or more officers or employees of the procuring entity who shall be responsible for communicating directly with bidders and for receiving direct communications from them relating to the procurement proceedings, as well as the job title and address of that officer or employee;

h- A reference to the right of complaint or review provided for in Chapter Seven of this Law regarding decisions or measures taken by the procuring entity that are alleged not to comply with the provisions of this Law;

i- Any formalities that become necessary once a winning proposal is accepted in order for the procurement contract to enter into force;

j- Any other requirements that may be determined by the procuring entity in conformity with this Law and its implementing decrees.

5- Prior to opening the envelopes containing the financial aspects of the proposals, the procuring entity shall examine and evaluate the technical, functional and performance characteristics of the proposals in accordance with the criteria and procedures specified in the request for proposals.

6- The results of the examination and evaluation of the technical, functional and performance characteristics of the proposals shall immediately be included in the record of the procurement proceedings.

7- Proposals whose technical, functional and performance characteristics do not meet the relevant minimum requirements shall be considered non-responsive to the requirements and shall be rejected on this ground. Every bidder whose proposal is rejected shall also be notified of the grounds for rejection, and the unopened envelope containing the financial aspects of the proposal shall be returned to it, after the completion of the award procedures.

8- Proposals whose technical, functional and performance specifications meet or exceed the relevant minimum requirements shall be considered substantially responsive to the requirements. The procuring entity shall notify every bidder that submitted a proposal substantially responsive to the requirements of the score achieved by the technical, functional and performance characteristics of its proposal. The procuring entity shall invite all such bidders to the session for opening the envelopes containing the financial aspects of their proposals.

9- The score achieved by the technical, functional and performance specifications of each proposal responsive to the requirements and the corresponding financial aspect of that proposal shall be read out in the presence of the bidders invited, in accordance with paragraph 8 of this Article, to the session for opening the envelopes containing the financial aspects of the proposals.

10- The procuring entity shall compare the financial aspects of the proposals responsive to the requirements and on that basis identify the winning proposal in accordance with the criteria and procedures set forth in the request for proposals. The winning proposal shall be the proposal that obtains the best combined evaluation in terms of the following: (a) the criteria other than price set forth in the request for proposals and (b) the price.

11- The procuring entity shall duly publish the result of the award.

Awarding of the framework agreement:

1- The procuring entity shall award the framework agreement by one of the following two means:

a- By means of public tender procedures, in accordance with the provisions of Section II of this Chapter, in a manner not inconsistent with the provisions of this Section;

b- By means of other procurement methods in accordance with the relevant provisions of Sections I, III and IV of this Chapter, in a manner not inconsistent with the provisions of this Section.

2- The provisions of this Law governing the contents of the invitation and pre-qualification, where adopted, shall apply to the information provided to bidders when they are invited for the first time to participate in framework agreement procedures, in a manner not inconsistent with the provisions of this Section. At that stage, the procuring entity shall indicate, in addition thereto, the following:

a- That the procurement shall be conducted as a framework agreement procedure;

b- Whether the framework agreement shall be concluded with one or more suppliers, contractors, consultants or service providers;

c- The minimum or maximum limit imposed on the number of suppliers, contractors, consultants or service providers who shall be parties to the framework agreement if the agreement is to be concluded with more than one supplier, contractor, consultant or service provider;

d- The form, terms and conditions of the framework agreement in accordance with Article 64 of this Law.

3- The provisions of Article 24 of this Law shall apply to the awarding of the framework agreement, mutatis mutandis.

Requirements relating to framework agreements:

1- A framework agreement shall be concluded in writing, and shall set out the following:

(a) the duration of the framework agreement, which shall not be less than one year and shall not exceed four years, and which is neither extensible nor renewable in any case and its terms may not be altered;

(b) a description of the subject matter of the procurement and all other terms and conditions of the procurement determined at the time of the conclusion of the framework agreement;

(c) estimates of the terms and conditions of the procurement that cannot be determined with sufficient precision at the time of the conclusion of the framework agreement, to the extent that they are known;

(d) whether a framework agreement concluded with more than one supplier, contractor, consultant or service provider shall involve a second-stage competition in order to award a procurement contract under the framework agreement, and if so:

1) a statement of the terms and conditions of the procurement that are to be established or refined during the second-stage competition;

2) the procedures for any second-stage competition, the anticipated frequency of such competition, and the envisaged deadlines for presenting second-stage submissions;

3) the procedures and criteria to be applied during the second-stage competition, including the relative weights of such criteria and the manner in which they will be applied, in accordance with Articles 17 and 18 of this Law. Where the relative weights of the evaluation criteria may vary during the second-stage competition, the framework agreement shall specify the permissible range of variation;

4) whether the procurement contract under the framework agreement shall be awarded to the lowest-priced or the most advantageous submission;

5) the manner in which the procurement contract shall be awarded.

2- A framework agreement with more than one supplier, contractor, consultant or service provider shall be concluded as one agreement between all the parties.

3- The framework agreement shall include, in addition to the other information specified in this Article, the information necessary to allow the effective operation of the framework agreement, including information on how the text of the agreement and notices of forthcoming procurement contracts thereunder may be accessed, and the necessary information relating to means of communication, including, without limitation, in the event of electronic communication.

Second stage of a framework agreement procedure:

1- Every procurement contract under the framework agreement shall be awarded in accordance with the terms and conditions of that agreement and in accordance with the provisions of this Article.

2- No procurement contract under the framework agreement may be awarded except to a supplier, contractor, consultant, or service provider that is a party to that agreement.

3- The provisions of Article 24 of this Law, with the exception of Paragraph 2 thereof, shall apply to the acceptance of the successful submission under framework agreements without second-stage competition.

4- In a framework agreement with second-stage competition, the following procedures shall apply to the award of a procurement contract:

a- The procuring entity shall issue a written invitation to present offers that is sent simultaneously so as to:

1) Include every supplier, contractor, consultant, or service provider that is a party to the framework agreement; or

2) Be restricted to the suppliers, contractors, consultants, or service providers that are parties to the framework agreement then capable of meeting the needs of that procuring entity with respect to the subject matter of the procurement, provided that at the same time a notice of the second-stage competition is sent to all parties to the framework agreement so that they have the opportunity to participate in the second-stage competition;

b- The invitation to present offers shall include the following information:

1) A statement restating the existing terms and conditions of the framework agreement to be included in the anticipated procurement contract, specifying the terms and conditions of the procurement that are subject to the second-stage competition, and providing further details of those terms and conditions as appropriate;

2) A statement restating the procedures and criteria for the award of the anticipated procurement contract, including the weighting and the manner in which they will be applied;

3) Instructions for preparing the offers;

4) The manner, place, and deadline for presenting the offers;

5) Where suppliers, contractors, consultants, or service providers are permitted to present offers for only a portion of the subject matter of the procurement, a description of the portion or portions for which offers may be presented;

6) The manner in which the offer price is to be formulated and expressed, including a statement of whether the price is to cover elements other than the cost of the subject matter of the procurement itself, such as any applicable customs duties and taxes;

7) References to this Law and its implementing decrees, and other laws and decrees directly pertinent to the procurement proceedings, including those applicable to procurement involving classified information, and the place where those laws and decrees may be found;

8) The name, functional title, and address of one or more officers or employees of the procuring entity who are authorized to communicate directly with and to receive communications directly from bidders in connection with the second-stage competition;

9) A notice of the right provided under Article 103 of this Law to complain or seek review of decisions and actions taken by the procuring entity that are allegedly non-compliant with the provisions of this Law, together with information on the duration of the applicable standstill period and, where none applies, a statement to that effect and the reasons therefor;

10) Any formalities that will be required once a successful offer has been accepted for the procurement contract to enter into force, including, where applicable, the execution of a written procurement contract pursuant to Article 24 of this Law;

11) Any other requirements established by the procuring entity in accordance with this Law and its implementing decrees relating to the preparation and submission of offers and to other aspects of the second-stage competition;

c- The procuring entity shall evaluate all offers received and determine the successful offer in accordance with the evaluation criteria and procedures set out in the invitation to present offers;

d- The procuring entity shall accept the successful offer in accordance with Article 24 of this Law.

Functions of the Public Procurement Authority:

The Authority shall be concerned with regulating, supervising and monitoring public procurement and developing its procedures, systems and performance, and shall also be concerned with coordinating among the various procuring entities and providing technical assistance and guidance thereto. It may perform the functions entrusted to it, including, by way of example and not limitation:

1- Proposing to the Council of Ministers general policies relating to procurement;

2- Designing, managing and operating the central electronic platform for public procurement and electronic procurement;

3- Collecting the annual procurement plans received from the procuring entities in accordance with a unified template issued by it, classifying them and publishing them in due form on the approved central electronic platform in accordance with Article 11 of this Law; provided that they include detailed information on the types, quantities, contracting methods and the concerned sectors in a manner that allows the market to prepare for competition;

4- Publishing all announcements and notices relating to procurements and to prequalification and awarding procedures in due form on the approved central electronic platform and obliging the procuring entities to do so;

5- Establishing and updating a list of supplies, services and simple works the centralised procurement of which may lead to achieving the best value from the expenditure of public funds, and proposing the entity that carries out centralised procurement for each type thereof. This list shall be approved by a decree adopted in the Council of Ministers upon the proposal of its President;

6- Expressing an opinion on the framework agreements referred to in Chapter Three of this Law;

7- Issuing decisions regarding the approval for the procuring entity to use the two-stage tendering method;

8- Issuing guidelines and clarifications on the legal texts in force relating to public procurement, including issuing manuals and guiding rules;

9- Monitoring and evaluating the application of the legal texts and rules governing public procurement, and in this context it shall be entitled to inspect the procurement record of the procuring entity, subject to data confidentiality in accordance with the provisions of Article 6 of this Law. The Authority shall prepare periodic reports addressing areas of deficiency and/or violation of laws in all procuring entities, submit them to the Presidency of the Republic, the Chamber of Deputies, the Council of Ministers, the Court of Accounts and the Central Inspection, and publish them in due form;

10- Referring any documents or instruments relating to a contract or procedure subject to the provisions of this Law to the competent criminal authority in the event of suspicion of the occurrence of any violation punishable under criminal laws, as well as requesting the referral of the employee around whose collusion a suspicion arises to the competent oversight authorities;

11- Collecting data and documents specific to public procurement operations, at all levels, on the central platform, including types and methods of contracting, participating economic sectors, and execution data of projects funded by donor entities and others, in a central database held by it, in a manner that allows ease of reading and analysing such data and documents and in accordance with the approved international standards in this field;

12- Keeping and updating records of public contracts and publishing them on the approved central electronic platform, subject to confidentiality with respect to public contracts relating to national security and defence and subject to the provisions of Article Six of this Law;

13- Keeping debarment decisions in a special public register and recording its remarks thereon, if any, and including them in its reports. The debarment register shall be published on the approved central electronic platform, and the Public Procurement Authority shall be responsible for updating this register in accordance with Article 40 of this Law;

- 14 Verifying the qualifications of the members of the awarding and acceptance committees at the procuring entities and including in its reports, where necessary, the extent to which these qualifications conform to the required conditions of experience and specialisation.

15- Processing and analysing information specific to public procurement operations, establishing and issuing performance standards, and preparing and publishing analytical and statistical reports;

16- Issuing standard documents and templates for public procurement procedures, including model bidding documents and model prequalification files, and making them available to the procuring entities for mandatory adoption;

17- Proposing regulations for the application of internal audit with respect to procurement procedures and operations at the procuring entities;

18- Establishing the code of conduct and professional ethics specific to public procurement operations provided for in Article 10 of this Law;

19- Establishing guidelines, handbooks and clarifications with respect to the rules and procedures of public procurement, providing advice, assistance and expertise to procuring entities and potential bidders, and making information available through the approved unified electronic platform;

20- Proposing the training policy relating to public procurement, taking into account the priorities and needs of the procuring entity, the available national expertise, and good international practices;

21- Submitting proposals and recommendations to the concerned entities on the means and ways of developing and encouraging innovation in public procurement, incentivising small and medium-sized enterprises to participate in procurement, improving applications, and proposing the amendment of regulations and laws;

22- Submitting recommendations relating to the amendment of the financial thresholds set out in this Law to the Council of Ministers through its President;

23- Surveying the opinion of the public and the private sector when proposing regulatory or legal amendments to the public procurement system and explaining the options that have been adopted by publishing draft amendments on the central electronic platform, by way of example and not limitation, and inviting the public to express proposals and remarks;

24- Cooperating with international institutions and other bodies locally and internationally to develop the public procurement system;

25- Preparing periodic reports where necessary and an annual report on the workflow of the Authority, which shall be communicated to the Chamber of Deputies, the Council of Ministers and the oversight entities, and published to the public.

Powers of the President of the Authority:

The President of the Authority shall be vested with the following powers:

1- Presiding over the administrative body in his capacity as President of the Authority and the hierarchical superior of those working therein;

2- Representing the Authority towards third parties and before the judiciary, and signing on its behalf;

3- Calling for extraordinary meetings when necessary, and preparing the agendas of all meetings, preparing for them and conducting them;

4- Operating the account of the Authority with the Central Bank of Lebanon jointly with the accountant or the financial director of the Authority, in accordance with the provisions of its financial regulations;

5- Ensuring the execution of decisions and the application of the relevant provisions in force, and managing the Authority and conducting its daily business;

6- Preparing a draft annual budget for the Authority in accordance with the laws and regulations in force;

7- Delegating, whenever the need arises, a representative of the Authority to attend the awarding sessions that take place in the procuring entity in the capacity of an observer, who shall not have the right to vote;

8- The right to lodge appeals before the Complaints Authority and applications for review before the State Council and any other competent authority;

9- Referring the drafts, opinions, recommendations, decisions and periodic reports provided for by the law to the competent constitutional, administrative and oversight authorities.

Contracting authorities:

The contracting authorities shall undertake the performance of their duties in relation to procurement and contracting procedures in accordance with the provisions of this Law, including:

1- Planning their public procurements and including their annual procurement plan in their budgets in a manner that ensures the availability of the necessary appropriations, and scheduling them within a multi-year framework where necessary;

2- Establishing mechanisms to study, evaluate and analyse their procurement needs as required by the provisions of this Law;

3- Sending information and data to the Public Procurement Authority as provided for by the provisions of this Law and its implementing decrees;

4- Complying with the publication of the information required pursuant to the provisions of this Law and its implementing decrees on their website, if any, and on the central electronic platform of the Public Procurement Authority in a manner that ensures transparency while observing the right of access to information;

5- Establishing clear mechanisms for preparing market studies as required by the provisions of this Law;

6- Preparing tender documents and making them available as required by the provisions of this Law;

7- Announcing successively their procurements in due form and in accordance with Article 12 of this Law and on the central electronic platform of the Public Procurement Authority;

8- Managing and following up on the execution of contracts in a manner that ensures achieving best value from the expenditure of public funds and preserving the public interest, as required by the provisions of this Law;

9- Establishing plans for risk management and for achieving sustainable development goals in public procurement;

10- Preparing an annual report on the procurement operations conducted in accordance with a template established by the Public Procurement Authority, and sending it to the Court of Audit and the Public Procurement Authority and publishing it in due form;

11- Other duties specified in this Law and in other laws and regulations.

Filing a complaint with the Challenges Authority:

1- Notwithstanding any other provision, complaints shall be filed exclusively with the Challenges Authority regarding any decision or measure taken by the procuring entity or any of the parties concerned with the procurement in the course of the procurement proceedings or regarding the failure of the procuring entity to issue a decision pursuant to Article 105 of this Law within the time limits provided for in that Article, accompanied by the legal and factual grounds on which they are based.

2- Complaints shall be filed in writing within the following time periods:

a- Prior to the deadline for the submission of tenders if they relate to the conditions of solicitation or pre-qualification or the decisions or measures taken by the procuring entity in this context;

b- Within the standstill period of //10// ten working days which commences from the date of notification of the winning tenderer (known as the provisional awardee) as provided for in Paragraph 2 of Article 24 of this Law, if they relate to other decisions or measures taken by the procuring entity within the framework of the procurement proceedings. In the event that no standstill period is applied, at any time prior to the entry into force of the procurement contract or the framework agreement.

c- Complaints regarding the failure of the procuring entity to issue a decision pursuant to Article 105 of this Law within the time limits specified in that Article shall be filed within a time limit of five //5// working days from the date on which the applicant for review ought to have been notified of the decision of the procuring entity in accordance with the provisions of Article 105 of this Law.

3- Upon receiving the complaint, the Challenges Authority shall immediately undertake the following:

a- Take a decision to suspend the procurement proceedings if it deems this necessary to protect the interests of the complainant and if the complaint is serious and based on significant grounds.

In this case, the suspension shall be for a duration of ten //10// working days in the event that it received the complaint prior to the deadline for the submission of tenders; the Challenges Authority may also extend or lift any applied suspension, subject to the provisions set forth in this Article;

b- Notify the procuring entity and all identified participants in the procurement proceedings to whom the complaint relates of the content of that complaint;

c- In the event that the Authority decides to suspend the proceedings, it shall specify the duration of the suspension and notify all identified participants in the procurement proceedings to whom the complaint relates of its decision regarding the suspension;

d- Publish a notice containing a summary of the subject matter of the complaint and its grounds on the central electronic platform of the Public Procurement Authority.

4- The Challenges Authority may dismiss the complaint if it decides that it is not based on legal or factual grounds, and it shall thereupon notify the complainant, the procuring entity and all other participants in the procurement proceedings of the dismissal and of the grounds therefor. Such dismissal shall constitute a decision on the complaint.

5- Notices shall be addressed to the complainant, the procuring entity and the other participants in the procurement proceedings pursuant to Paragraphs 3 and 4 of this Article, no later than three //3// working days following the issuance of the decisions relating thereto.

6- The procuring entity shall, immediately upon receiving a notice addressed pursuant to Sub-paragraph 3 (b) of this Article, enable the Challenges Authority to have effective access to all documents in its possession relating to the procurement proceedings.

7- The Authority shall take one or more of the following measures, as appropriate:

a- Obliging the procuring entity and other concerned parties to refrain from any action that violates the provisions of this Law;

b- Annulling in whole or in part the act or decision of the procuring entity that does not comply with the provisions of this Law within the pre-contractual framework;

c- Obliging the procuring entity that has followed procedures violating the provisions of this Law to rectify them and also to rectify any decision issued by it that does not comply with the provisions of this Law within the pre-contractual framework;

d- Confirming any decision issued by the procuring entity;

e- Terminating the procurement proceedings;

f- Dismissing the complaint;

g- Taking alternative measures as required by the circumstances.

8- The Challenges Authority shall notify the Court of Audit and the Public Prosecution Office attached thereto, the Public Procurement Authority, and the Central Inspection of the violations discovered by it when adjudicating the complaint, in order to take the necessary measures.

9- The decision of the Challenges Authority pursuant to Paragraph 7 of this Article shall be issued within a time limit of //20// twenty working days following the receipt of the complaint. The Authority shall immediately thereafter notify the decision to the procuring entity and the complainant, and to all other participants in the challenge proceedings and in the procurement proceedings. Upon the issuance of the decision, any suspension of the procurement proceedings that may have been decided by the Challenges Authority shall lapse.

10- All decisions issued by the Challenges Authority pursuant to this Article, with the exception of implicit decisions of dismissal, shall be written and reasoned decisions stating the measures taken and the grounds for taking them. The procuring entity shall include in the record of procurement proceedings provided for in Article 9 of this Law the following:

a- The complaint received by the Challenges Authority pursuant to this Article;

b- The decisions issued by the Challenges Authority.

11- The decisions of the Challenges Authority shall be subject to appeal before the State Council within seven working days from the date of their notification, and the expiry of the time limit specified in Paragraph 9 of this Article and the silence maintained by the Authority regarding the application submitted to it, or the abstention from notifying its decision in accordance with the provisions of this Law, shall constitute an implicit decision of dismissal which shall in turn be subject to appeal before the State Council.

12- The filing of the complaint shall freeze any oversight procedures, where they exist, until the complaint is adjudicated and a decision is issued thereon.

13- Where the decision of the Authority is contravened by any other oversight body, the decision of the latter must be justified and published on the central electronic platform in order to become effective.

Penalties:

In addition to the penalties provided for in this Law and other laws, in particular the Criminal Code, the following penalties shall apply:

First: Criminal Penalties:

1- Anyone who violates the provisions of the first paragraph of Article 110 of this Law shall be punished by imprisonment from two months to two years, and by a fine from twenty times to two hundred times the minimum wage in force on the date of the violation, or by one of these two penalties. The fine shall be doubled in the event of recidivism.

2- Anyone who violates the provisions of Paragraph 5 of Article 110 shall be punished by imprisonment for a period of not less than two years and by a fine ranging between two times and three times the expected or realized material benefit, and where it is not possible to accurately determine the value of the expected material benefit, a fine ranging between twenty times and two hundred times the minimum wage in force on the date of the violation shall apply.

3- The employee or supervisor and anyone who participated in supervising and receiving the executed services, supplies, or works shall be punished by imprisonment from one year to three years and by a fine ranging between two times and three times the value of the part of the contract whose execution, receipt, or supervision he is responsible for monitoring, if he overlooks recording the committed violations, breaches the execution of the terms of the contract, neglects monitoring, delays taking the measures required to be taken, or fails to perform his functional obligations in accordance with the rules, each according to his responsibility. The penalty shall be aggravated in the event of recidivism.

4- The Criminal Code and the laws relating to combating corruption, in particular the Law on Combating Corruption in the Public Sector and Establishing the National Anti-Corruption Institution No. 175/2020 dated 8/5/2020 and its amendments, the Law on Declaration of Assets and Interests and Punishment of Illicit Enrichment No. 189 dated 16/10/2020 and its amendments, and all other relevant laws shall apply to the offences that fall under their scope and are related to the implementation of this Law. In the event of conflict among these laws, the severest penalty shall apply against the prosecuted persons.

5- The accomplice, instigator, accessory, and beneficiary shall be punished by the same penalty prescribed for the principal offender in the offences set forth above.

6- A contractor who is a legal person shall be held criminally liable for the acts of its directors, management members, representatives, and workers when they commit such acts in the name of the said legal person or through one of its means, in accordance with the provisions of Article 210 of the Criminal Code.

Second: Disciplinary and Financial Penalties:

The Court of Accounts and the Central Inspection Administration shall prosecute, each within the scope of its competence, decision-makers, employees, and workers at all procuring entities and other bodies competent in public procurement.

Third: Financial Fines:

1- The Court of Accounts shall, of its own motion or at the request of the Public Procurement Authority, impose fines against procuring entities and obligate them to pay a financial fine ranging between twenty times and two hundred times the minimum wage in force on the date of the violation, according to the scale and gravity of the violation, in the event of their violation of the provisions provided for in this Law and its implementing regulations, in particular those relating to the following:

a- violating the procurement procedures provided for in this Law;

b- splitting procurement contrary to the law;

c- violating anti-corruption provisions when conducting public procurement operations;

d- failing to take the appropriate measure to avoid, identify, and prevent conflict of interest in procurement procedures;

e- failing to comply with the rules for adopting legal procurement methods;

f- violating the rules of publication and announcement as provided for by the provisions of this Law;

g- failing to file with the Public Procurement Authority, the oversight bodies, and the Complaints Authority the required information and documents in accordance with the rules;

h- failing to comply with the planning obligation provided for in Article 11 of this Law;

i- failing to comply with the legal time limits for announcement, submission of bids, and receipt, and failing to observe the standstill period;

j- violating the time limits of the rules for lodging bid and performance securities;

k- awarding the contract to a bid that does not meet the required conditions or does not constitute the best of the submitted bids;

l- cancelling the procurement contrary to the rules specified in this Law;

m- failing to establish the procurement record provided for in Article 9 of this Law or entering information therein contrary to the rules;

n- failing to draw up the annual report provided for in Article 102 of this Law;

o- committing errors in registering submitted bids;

p- obstructing bidders' access to legally available information and documents;

q- amending the terms and specifications before and after contracting contrary to the provisions of this Law.

2- The contractor shall be administratively obligated by the Public Procurement Authority to pay a financial fine ranging between twenty times and two hundred times the minimum wage in force on the date of the violation, upon violating the provisions of this Law and its implementing decrees.

3- The procuring entity and the contractor shall have the right to challenge fining decisions before the State Council.

Fourth: Abuse of the Right to Object

1- A bidder who abuses his right to object provided for in Chapter Seven of this Law shall be punished by a financial fine ranging between twenty times and two hundred times the minimum wage in force on the date of the abuse of the right to object.

2- The Public Procurement Authority shall impose this fine at the request of the Complaints Authority.

3- Abuse of the right shall be established when submitted objections are rejected three consecutive times, or when it is established that the objection was submitted merely for procrastination or with manifest lack of seriousness.

4- When the fine is imposed by the Public Procurement Authority in accordance with this clause, the procuring entity shall apply the debarment procedures provided for in Article 40 of this Law.

5- The contractor shall have the right to request to challenge fining and debarment decisions before the State Council.
